WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.560
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got moose wearing GPS collars across Tŝilhqot'in territory, hunters being asked to pass on them, and the real story underneath that — a wildlife monitoring program running under the Nation's own declared law. Plus the question Casey won't let go of: once a collar can find the animal for you, does anyone still need to learn how to track it? Coming right up.

00:00:40.035 --> 00:01:02.291
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Thursday, September 10th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:13.499
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at who's actually holding the tools that watch over this territory, and what travels along with them when the tool gets easier to use than the skill it replaces.

00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:32.279
<v Casey>Quick weather check — out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's drizzly, around 8 degrees, with a high of 15 and a 78 percent chance of rain, about a millimetre expected. Clear skies everywhere else worth mentioning: 100 Mile House around 7, Williams Lake and Quesnel both sitting around 9, and Tatla Lake around 8.

00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:35.040
<v Riley>Let's get into what's happening around the region.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:02:06.208
<v Riley>Nine stories close to home this morning, a full slate, so we'll move with some pace. Starting in Williams Lake — city council got an update this week on the Boitanio Mall residential redevelopment. Janda Group, the developer, is now targeting a commissioning completion in spring of 2027, converting part of the old mall footprint into housing units. Council received the progress report at its regular meeting, and the timeline holds steady from earlier projections.

00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:12.782
<v Casey>Housing stock in a market that's added almost nothing new in years. We'll take whatever gets built.

00:02:13.973 --> 00:02:40.373
<v Riley>Quesnel is stepping back from something on the animal-control side. City council confirmed this week that bylaw officers will no longer pick up stray animals within city limits. The reasoning, as presented to council, was about prioritizing limited resources toward the most vulnerable animals — but the immediate cause is that the SPCA declined to renew its kenneling services contract with the city, effective the end of December.

00:02:40.273 --> 00:02:44.577
<v Casey>Which means somebody else absorbs that gap. A rescue group, a neighbour, nobody.

00:02:45.182 --> 00:02:48.126
<v Riley>That's the open question nobody at that meeting seemed to answer.

00:02:49.369 --> 00:03:12.769
<v Casey>Staying in the territory — the Tŝilhqot'in National Government has a public notice out ahead of this hunting season. Don't shoot the moose wearing GPS collars. TNG's release explains the collars are part of an ongoing wildlife monitoring effort tracking herd movement and population health across Tŝilhqot'in territory, and it's asking hunters heading into the field to recognize the collars and pass on those animals.

00:03:13.347 --> 00:03:15.835
<v Riley>We'll spend more time with that program in a few minutes.

00:03:17.056 --> 00:03:39.336
<v Casey>Two fires to flag quickly. The Quesnel, Kersley, and Barlow Creek volunteer fire departments all responded to a structure fire on Quartz Avenue on the morning of September 9th — described as an all-hands response involving all three departments. Separately, in Vernon, police have made an arrest in connection with a house fire on BX Road; a 23-year-old is now in custody following that blaze.

00:03:40.609 --> 00:04:04.337
<v Riley>In the South Cariboo, there's a new name on the October municipal ballot. Realtor David Jurek, who moved to the area a decade ago and owns the local RE/MAX franchise, is running for mayor of 100 Mile House, challenging incumbent Maureen Pinkney for the district's top job. Jurek's pitch centres on bringing people together around good ideas and, in his words, actually getting things done.

00:04:04.916 --> 00:04:07.644
<v Casey>We'll see if voters think that's what's been missing from council.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:30.494
<v Casey>B.C.'s forestry sector says it's reached what industry groups are calling a breaking point. The province's forests minister points to tariffs as the main driver of the crisis. Industry associations pushed back on that framing, arguing the deeper problems are homegrown — permitting delays, fibre supply constraints, and mill closures that predate the current trade dispute by years.

00:04:31.965 --> 00:04:38.957
<v Riley>Those two things aren't in conflict. Tariffs made an already fragile system worse. They didn't create the fragility.

00:04:40.525 --> 00:05:03.917
<v Riley>Near Rossland, a discharge permit for a proposed magnesium mine is drawing organized local opposition. Residents near Record Ridge have raised concerns about water quality impacts from the operation moving forward under that permit. It's shaping up as a preview of the kind of permitting fights likely to recur across B.C. as more critical-minerals projects move through the approval pipeline.

00:05:05.343 --> 00:05:21.503
<v Casey>And the province put out funding this week aimed specifically at strengthening public emergency notification systems and evacuation-route planning across communities — a response, at least in part, to how badly some of those systems performed during recent wildfire seasons in this region.

00:05:22.821 --> 00:05:31.109
<v Riley>Communities that had their notification systems tested by an actual fire this summer will tell you that funding is years overdue, not early.

00:05:32.308 --> 00:06:06.236
<v Casey>A First Nations woman from Sucker Creek First Nation needs a liver transplant, and her family says an Edmonton hospital isn't providing adequate care. She's been travelling between Brandon and Winnipeg for treatment because of where she can access it, in and out of hospital monthly. Her daughter told reporters she runs out of energy fast, sleeps most of the day from discomfort, and can't be present as a mother the way she wants to be. The family frames this as a pattern they've encountered before — Indigenous patients receiving slower, thinner care than non-Indigenous patients with comparable conditions.

00:06:07.562 --> 00:06:13.802
<v Riley>That pattern keeps surfacing every time someone actually studies referral timelines broken down by ethnicity.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:17.734
<v Casey>It surfaces. Nobody's tracked whether it's actually being fixed.

00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:43.870
<v Riley>Further out — Britain has banned imports from Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank, and eleven other countries, including Canada, have joined comparable measures. Mother Jones frames the U.S. as increasingly isolated on the issue, sitting outside a coalition that now includes most of its traditional allies. The ban applies specifically to goods produced within settlements, not broader trade with Israel.

00:06:45.166 --> 00:07:08.246
<v Casey>The Atlantic has a long feature out on commercial surrogacy in the United States, an industry the piece describes as essentially unregulated at the federal level, even as most of Europe bans the practice outright and the United Nations has compared elements of it to trafficking. It's a striking contrast in how different governments decide whose bodily autonomy gets legal protection and whose gets a contract and a price.

00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:36.837
<v Riley>And a flag specifically for our American listeners, not something Canadians have a vote in — Mother Jones is tracking what it describes as a decade-long pattern of Russian interference targeting U.S. elections, with fresh reporting ahead of this year's midterms. Worth watching from up here mainly as a case study in what happens once election security failures compound over multiple cycles rather than get addressed after the first one.

00:07:38.208 --> 00:08:01.136
<v Casey>ArchDaily published a piece this week on urban design and women's safety, examining four public spaces internationally and how lighting, sightlines, and layout change how safe a space feels to move through alone. It's a design question that applies as much to a parking lot behind a downtown Quesnel business as it does to a plaza in Johannesburg — sightlines and lighting cost real budget line items either way.

00:08:02.629 --> 00:08:25.693
<v Riley>And to close the loop globally — the United Nations released what's being called one of its bleakest climate reports yet this week, effectively conceding the world will exceed the Paris Agreement's safe temperature threshold. The report shifts its emphasis toward adaptation strategies rather than prevention, which is a notable tonal change from earlier UN climate messaging.

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:28.351
<v Casey>Cheerful way to head into a spotlight segment.

00:08:28.897 --> 00:08:31.697
<v Riley>We'll carry that moose collar story straight into the Deep Dive.

00:08:33.097 --> 00:09:03.233
<v Casey>Today's spotlight goes to the Williams Lake Indian Band, known as T'exelcemc — a self-governing First Nation running programs across language revitalization, housing, and land stewardship. Their work spans bringing fluent Secwepemctsín speakers into classrooms, managing housing development on their own terms rather than waiting on outside developers, and stewarding territory that's been cared for by their people long before any provincial or municipal boundary existed on a map. You can find out more about their current programs at wlfn.ca.

00:09:03.885 --> 00:09:08.525
<v Riley>Self-governing is the word to sit with there. It's going to come up again in a few minutes.

00:09:09.925 --> 00:09:28.877
<v Riley>Start with the moose collars. The Tŝilhqot'in National Government is asking hunters not to shoot animals wearing GPS trackers this season, which tells you a monitoring program is running right now across a stretch of territory nobody could cover on foot at that scale — tracking herd movement, health, population patterns.

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:32.737
<v Casey>So the real question is what that program is actually replacing.

00:09:33.383 --> 00:09:48.255
<v Riley>It's Tŝilhqot'in-run wildlife monitoring. That's the important part. It's not a provincial biologist flying in once a season to count antlers from a helicopter. It's the Nation's own guardians deciding what gets tracked, where, and why it matters to them.

00:09:48.855 --> 00:10:17.479
<v Casey>The thing I keep turning over, though: a guardian used to mean someone who could read moose sign in October snow — track width, browse patterns, where the bulls hold up during rut. That's knowledge that took a person twenty years of walking that ground to build. And it only ever passed one way — from someone who had it, to someone willing to walk beside them long enough to earn it. Once a collar tells you exactly where the animal is, does the next generation of guardians still need to learn to read the ground the old way?

00:10:17.767 --> 00:10:24.671
<v Riley>That's the real question for today, isn't it. What happens to a skill once the tool that used to require it stops requiring it.

00:10:24.771 --> 00:10:56.843
<v Riley>I don't think the collar replaces the tracker, though. It extends what one guardian can cover. A person on foot can walk maybe fifteen kilometres of terrain in a day, in good conditions. A dozen collared animals give you movement data across an entire territory, through every season, without anyone needing to physically be in twelve places at once. The guardians still go out to confirm what the data shows. A collar tells you an animal stopped moving for six hours. It doesn't tell you why. Somebody still has to hike in and look.

00:10:57.388 --> 00:11:11.396
<v Casey>Somebody has to hike in and look this year, while the people who trained today's guardians are still alive to send them out. What happens in fifteen years, when a dashboard alert is the only training a new guardian ever gets before they're sent into the bush?

00:11:11.728 --> 00:11:34.192
<v Riley>Who designs the training pipeline matters more than the hardware itself. Guardian programs elsewhere in Canada have built the technology into an apprenticeship structure rather than letting it substitute for one — new hires spend a full season on the land alongside an elder or a senior guardian before they're trusted to interpret what a collar alert is actually telling them.

00:11:34.813 --> 00:11:45.725
<v Casey>"Elsewhere" is doing the reassuring in that sentence. Is that documented specifically for the Tŝilhqot'in program, or is that a general pattern borrowed from other guardian networks across the country?

00:11:46.015 --> 00:12:15.119
<v Riley>Fair to call that out. It's the general pattern. TNG's internal training structure for this specific collar program isn't in front of me. That's a real gap in what I can claim. What is public is that this program sits inside TNG's own governance — under Dechen Ts'edilhtan, their declared laws — not under a provincial wildlife branch's authority. Whoever built the training pipeline for these guardians, they built it under Tŝilhqot'in jurisdiction, not against someone else's grant conditions.

00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:37.199
<v Casey>That distinction matters more than the tracking technology itself. A program built under a Nation's own declared law doesn't disappear the moment a funding cycle lapses, because its existence was never contingent on that funding cycle in the first place. Grant-funded pilot programs vanish constantly. Legally asserted jurisdiction tends to persist, whatever the budget year looks like.

00:12:37.710 --> 00:13:02.230
<v Riley>Compare that, sideways, to something happening in cartography right now. The UN just voted to phase out the standard Mercator projection because it shrinks Africa on nearly every map most of us grew up with — the continent looks smaller than Greenland on Mercator, when it's actually about fourteen times larger. Whoever draws the map decides what looks big, what looks small, what registers as significant.

00:13:02.827 --> 00:13:09.763
<v Casey>And whoever draws the map of Tŝilhqot'in territory decides what counts as evidence, in court and everywhere else that a map gets cited.

00:13:10.336 --> 00:13:34.504
<v Riley>The 2014 Tsilhqot'in Nation title case leaned on exactly that kind of evidence. Land-use maps the Nation had produced themselves, showing sustained and exclusive occupation over generations, going back decades before the case ever reached the Supreme Court. That case took twenty-five years to work through the courts, and the maps the Nation controlled were part of what ultimately won it.

00:13:35.032 --> 00:13:45.864
<v Casey>So the throughline here isn't the collar at all. It's who's holding the pen when the map gets drawn, whether that map is built from moose telemetry data or historical hunting-territory boundaries.

00:13:46.364 --> 00:14:01.292
<v Riley>Which is the difference between data collection as extraction and data collection as jurisdiction. TNG isn't asking anyone's permission to track those animals. They're asserting authority over their own territory, and the collar is incidental to that authority.

00:14:01.834 --> 00:14:23.874
<v Casey>Incidental until the collar's battery dies, or the vendor that supplies the hardware stops supporting the model, or the software that reads the signal gets bought out by a bigger company with different licensing terms. Somebody at TNG now has a maintenance line item in a budget they didn't need a decade ago, and that's a real dependency, even with the legal authority fully in Tŝilhqot'in hands.

00:14:24.396 --> 00:14:44.580
<v Riley>True, but the same argument applies to a paper map and a truck. Everything wears out eventually — welcome to owning anything with a battery in it. The question isn't whether dependency exists. It's whether the Nation controls the procurement decision when the replacement gets bought, or whether someone external controls it for them.

00:14:45.254 --> 00:14:54.470
<v Casey>Does TNG control that procurement, though? Or is a wildlife telemetry vendor somewhere setting the terms for what format they get their own tracking data handed back in?

00:14:54.731 --> 00:15:06.859
<v Riley>I don't have that contract in front of me either. That's a sharper open question than asking whether the technology works. It's asking who's the customer in that relationship and who's the vendor.

00:15:06.759 --> 00:15:08.607
<v Casey>Now we're actually getting somewhere.

00:15:09.306 --> 00:15:30.314
<v Riley>The Williams Lake Indian Band spotlight touches something similar from a different angle. Language revitalization programs, T'exelcemc's included, increasingly lean on apps, recording archives, digital dictionaries — tools that make it easier for someone to start learning Secwepemctsín outside a classroom, without a fluent speaker physically present in the room.

00:15:30.939 --> 00:15:49.659
<v Casey>And the number of fluent first-language speakers keeps shrinking every year, regardless of how polished the app gets. An app drills vocabulary well. It can't replicate what happens when a language gets spoken around a fire, in context, with the cadence and the joke and the correction that a screen strips clean out of the exchange.

00:15:50.234 --> 00:16:07.794
<v Riley>Sure — but the realistic alternative to the app isn't more fireside immersion. The pool of fluent speakers available for that kind of immersion keeps getting smaller and older every year. The realistic choice is app plus whatever direct contact with speakers still exists, not app instead of direct contact.

00:16:08.317 --> 00:16:20.965
<v Casey>Then the app's actual job is triage, not revitalization. It buys time while the harder problem — not enough fluent speakers left to teach everyone who wants to learn — doesn't get any easier no matter how good the interface gets.

00:16:21.594 --> 00:16:33.002
<v Riley>The tool doesn't solve the shortage of speakers. It manages the shortage while something else, presumably more fluent speakers actively teaching more learners, tries to close the gap underneath it.

00:16:33.674 --> 00:16:47.458
<v Casey>And who's funding that direct-teaching side, five years after the app's original grant cycle wraps up? That's usually where these programs quietly stall out — the flashy pilot gets photographed, the ongoing human labour underneath it doesn't get renewed.

00:16:47.958 --> 00:17:16.142
<v Riley>Which loops back to the guardian program. The visible, fundable piece — collars, apps, dashboards — is easy to put in a press release. The apprenticeship, the elder walking a young guardian through an entire season on the land, the fluent speaker correcting a learner's pronunciation for the hundredth patient time — that's the unglamorous part nobody photographs, and it's the part that actually determines whether any of this lasts past one funding cycle.

00:17:16.693 --> 00:17:30.869
<v Casey>So we actually agree on the failure mode here. We just disagree on how much blame belongs to the technology itself, versus the technology just being an easy target when the harder problem is people, time, and sustained money going into training the next generation directly.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:42.936
<v Riley>I'll take that framing. The collar isn't the villain in this story. Underfunding the human side of guardianship, while the tech side photographs well for a funding report, is the actual risk.

00:17:43.247 --> 00:17:46.319
<v Casey>And nobody's tracking that particular risk with a GPS collar.

00:17:46.631 --> 00:17:47.711
<v Riley>No argument there.

00:17:48.311 --> 00:18:07.183
<v Casey>If there's a concrete thing worth doing here, it's this — someone at TNG or at Williams Lake Indian Band publishing what their actual guardian and language apprenticeship pipelines look like on paper, not just the tech tools attached to them, would let outside observers stop guessing at the gap between the press release and the actual training room.

00:18:07.823 --> 00:18:22.167
<v Riley>And it would give other Nations building comparable programs something real to compare their own structures against, instead of every guardian program reinventing the apprenticeship model from scratch, alone, with no shared reference point.

00:18:22.777 --> 00:18:33.609
<v Casey>The real structural question is whether the apprenticeship behind it is funded on the same multi-year horizon as the hardware contract, or whether one of those two things quietly runs out first.

00:18:34.235 --> 00:18:42.947
<v Riley>If anyone listening knows more about how that training actually runs, on either front, we'd want to hear it — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:18:43.584 --> 00:18:49.152
<v Casey>We mean that specifically. This is one where the public record runs out faster than the questions do.

00:18:49.457 --> 00:18:50.729
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.

00:18:50.629 --> 00:18:54.013
<v Casey>Go read a map sometime. See who drew it, and what got left small.

00:18:54.113 --> 00:19:08.601
<v Casey>Look, I'll be straight with you — we didn't have anyone from First Nations communications in the room for this one, and that's something we should fix. If you're working in that space and want to come talk about what's actually happening on the ground, we're here for it.